## Three desks, not a survey

1. **Socratic cases.** A successful old sky watch does not establish moral expertise. A repayment claim meets both an ordinary grain return and a threatening weapon demand, in either order. The ruler-error objection elicits an intelligible expert-ruler reply rather than a refutation trophy.
2. **Plato and Diogenes.** The actual preceding expert-ruler reply is tested against a healing physician and a profit-seeking shepherd. Plato’s positive proposal remains beside its rival. Diogenes’ reported request tests a particular dependence on patronage, not all social dependence.
3. **Marcus.** Earlier independence is bounded by someone else’s hunger. The actual earlier repayment counterexample changes an imperial order. Judgment and correction exercises have a practical consequence. Providence and atoms remain an optional unresolved comparison.

There is no mandatory final “combine all conclusions” step. The retained Cephalus manuscript is distinguished from revised working annotations. One-use working claims can transform; source documents and live rival explanations can remain. The model determines actual retention where an operand has other authored uses.

Earlier-created ideas are not resupplied. Mandatory uses are the watch calendar; expert-ruler reply; and qualified repayment plus independent-need result. Bounded expertise has a genuinely optional later use. Greek/Babylonian and Cynic/Roman case comparisons are explicitly modern teaching links, **not claimed historical transmission**. See `RESEARCH.md`.

